Package: com.example.multifloat
Name: MultiFloat
Version: 0.20.0
Architecture: iphoneos-arm64e
Description: Multi-floating window / split-view tweak for iOS 17 (roothide / arm64e). 0.20.0 fixes the enlarged frozen-picture artifact reported with archive 21. The snapshot lifecycle fixes from 0.19.0 work (pad rebuilt on every app switch, raise on every real-black tick, lower after 2 stable healthy ticks - all confirmed in the log), but the corner grip pan gesture keeps catching the user's vertical home-gesture swipes that pass over the bottom-right grip: archive 21 shows the content area bouncing 448-605-367-432-340-416-526 while the user only switched apps, each bounce pushing a wrong height into scene.frame via syncContentGeometry, so the suspended app's frozen last frame (and the 2s-throttled snapshot) render stretched and frozen. The resize handler now records the frame at gesture start, and when within 0.8 s the size change is almost purely vertical (dy over 60 pt and dy over 3*dx - a fast swipe, not a deliberate diagonal grip drag) it immediately rolls the window frame back, disables and re-enables the recognizer, and skips syncContentGeometry entirely so scene.frame is never polluted. All eight logged bounces match the swipe signature and would be caught or are harmless. No new config keys.  0.19.0 fixes three lifecycle bugs in the 0.18.0 snapshot raise-and-mask mechanism, found by cross-reading archive 20: (A) attachContentView's clearContentArea removed the pad snapshot view from the content area on every app switch; the object stayed alive via its static reference, so the update path never rebuilt it (its guard only checked for nil) and the raise path silently early-returned on superview == nil - after the first app switch all 15 real-black episodes in the coolapk session had zero masking, which matches the user seeing a white flash on every third-party switch while system apps only flashed the first time. (B) The lower-back decision was re-scheduled to ticks+2 by every healthy tick, so under rapid back-and-forth switching it was postponed forever (observed: raised for 8 seconds spanning 7 drops), during which the raised flag blocked re-raising. It is now a consecutive-healthy-tick counter, reset by every real-black tick, lowering after 2 stable healthy ticks. (C) Raising was idempotent-once, but the system re-inserts the presentation view above the snapshot each time hosting resumes, so the snapshot must re-assert bringSubviewToFront on every real-black tick, not once. No new config keys; config version stays 19.  0.18.0 closes the remaining per-switch flash with a snapshot raise-and-mask mechanism. Archive 19 confirms the 0.17.0 mechanics all work as designed (preventive foreground keep-alive off, no-delta the dominant preventive outcome, geometry writes down from 27 to 2, tier escalation confirmed), yet the user still sees a flash on every single switch out and back. The archive pins the structural reason: every app switch drops the scene layers outright (47 real-black episodes, one per switch in both directions), the level-1 recovery write is asynchronous (400 to 800 ms), and the snapshot pad sitting at the bottom of the content area cannot mask flicker coming from the presentation view itself, which sits on top of the pad and can show black, white or a half-rendered frame during the resubmission. 0.18.0 therefore raises the snapshot to the top of the content area at the first tick a real black is detected, so the user sees the last healthy frozen frame instead of any flicker, and lowers it back to the bottom two ticks after recovery is confirmed by the render server (hosted layers back and contentState 2), so the real content covers the pad again. The pad keeps userInteractionEnabled NO, so touches pass through while it masks. The raise is skipped during the mount grace period, where the frozen frame would show stale content. New config key keepAliveSnapshotMask = true (false reverts to the pure bottom pad). Config version migrates 18 to 19.
